@bluebill1049 Should we allow devs to control whether to control `dirty` when we call `setValue`?
The purpose of "dirty" is mainly to check if user has entered a new value to a field or not, not to check if devs programmatically change that value, imo. If devs do call `setValue`, they can always save a boolean state to know if their form is dirty.

Let's say in the use case I fetch data from a server, and then I set that data as the default value of a field. In this case, `defaultValue` doesn't work because I don't have data before calling the API so I have to use `setValue` to manually change the value of the field. Using `value` instead of `defaultValue` is out of the question as controlled components are not optimal.

I tried to use `reset` but it doesn't seem to work with a TextField Select from MUI, which `setValue` seems to work with. This is the sample of my code:
````
<Controller
      name={name}
      control={control}
      as={
        <TextField select name={name}>
          {children}
        </TextField>
      }
      // Must have defaultValue to be '' so that react-hooks-form's "setValue()" works
      defaultValue={''}
      onChange={([a]) => a.target.value}
/>
````
`setValue` works if I set `defaultValue` as an empty string instead of skipping it. However, `reset` doesn't work with it. It does work with other uncontrolled fields though.

